# 4. PRODUCT ARCHITECTURE

WePay uses one unified collection engine with three collection modes.

You must understand the differences between them and design the interfaces accordingly.

## MODE 1: POOL — Bill Split & Group Pool

Purpose: Collect money towards a defined financial target.

Example:

Five friends are splitting a ₦75,000 Starlink subscription.

The organizer creates a POOL collection with a target of ₦75,000.

Contributors make payments until the target is reached.

Required interface elements:

- Collection title.
- Description.
- Target amount.
- Amount raised.
- Amount remaining.
- Contribution progress bar.
- Percentage completed.
- Number of contributors, if available.
- Contribution amount.
- Organizer information.
- Contributor payment form.
- Collection status.

The collection should visually communicate progress towards the target.

For example:

Target: ₦75,000
Amount raised: ₦45,000
Remaining: ₦30,000
Progress: 60%

Do not hardcode these values into the actual application logic. They are illustrative examples.

## MODE 2: OPEN — Open Collection / Dues

Purpose: Collect contributions without a hard financial target.

Example:

A departmental representative collects dues from students.

The organizer may configure:

- A fixed contribution amount.
- A minimum contribution amount.
- An optional deadline.
- Variable contributions where supported.

Required interface elements:

- Collection title.
- Description.
- Organizer information.
- Contribution amount or minimum amount.
- Optional deadline.
- Contributor form.
- Payment button.
- Collection status.

Unlike POOL, OPEN collections should not require a target progress bar.

## MODE 3: INVENTORY — Merchant Pre-Order

Purpose: Sell products with limited available stock.

Example:

A baker has 30 boxes of snacks available for pre-order.

Required interface elements:

- Product title.
- Product description.
- Unit price.
- Stock limit.
- Units sold.
- Remaining stock.
- Quantity selector.
- Contributor information form.
- Custom fulfillment fields.
- Total price calculation.
- Payment button.
- Availability status.

Example custom fields:

- Hostel Room.
- Matric Number.
- Delivery Location.

The interface must communicate stock availability clearly.

Do not allow the interface to imply that unavailable stock can be purchased.

